  "scientific_interpretation": {
    "raw_G02_result": "All8 centers show the required consecutive second-order behavior; all8 nevertheless fail the unchanged finest-h residual thresholds. FD_VALIDATION_UNRESOLVED is correct.",
    "raw_error_origin_supported_inference": "Large raw discrepancy is consistent with and strongly indicative of dominant central-stencil truncation over these h values: near-second-order raw decay and near-fourth-order D-independent Richardson differences are observed; higher-order extrapolated discrepancies drop by many orders. This does not prove exact D or independently bound shared operator error.",
    "R8_limit": "Final residual approximately2.1e-14 to4.65e-14 is only about1.4 to3.1 times the heuristic1.51e-14 epsilon-relative sample sensitivity. Treat as a sensitivity-scale result, not a certified floor or evidence of eighth-order convergence; one R8 value per center gives no R8 contraction sequence.",
    "operator_limit": "Adjacent spatial-resolution agreement is not a rigorous S/D error bound. Shared radial/quadrature/model errors and near-zero component noise remain possible.",
    "component_floor_limit": "At the raw finest stencil, normalized max entries are driven by roughly1.64e-14 absolute errors divided by the1e-12 floor. Those entries alone do not demonstrate a macroscopic derivative error. The raw spectral and Frobenius residual failures are separate and decisive.",
    "gate_policy": "Keep the original raw result unchanged. Richardson is an additional result-blind diagnostic and does not convert the old raw gate to PASS.",
    "global_component_gate_structure": "The code uses (global_max_abs<=1e-12) OR (global_max_normalized<=1e-6), rather than an entrywise mixed absolute/relative criterion. A near-zero entry can fail the global relative branch while a different truncation-dominated entry fails the global absolute branch. Preserve this original gate unchanged; any redesigned criterion needs a separate explicit scientific contract and cannot retroactively pass this run."
  },
  "recommended_next_step": {
    "priority_1_existing_data_only": "Inspect worst normalized component and block contribution before any further native h runs. The normalized maximum is the projectile same-center (9,13) entry; saved overlaps are identical at all four shifted pairs, so FD entry is exactly zero while D[9,13]+conj(D[13,9]) is approximately1.64e-14. This persistent cancellation residual is independent of shrinking h on the existing ladder.",
    "priority_2_error_budget_design": "Quantify saved S and analytic D arithmetic/quadrature sensitivity separately, including cancellation of opposite O(0.1) D entries and phase-oscillation truncation in cross blocks. A phase-aware overlap derivative or a higher-precision independently evaluated S/D check requires a new source/numerical contract. Do not simply reinterpret the heuristic epsilon sample surrogate as a certified bound.",
    "priority_3_new_native_only_if_discriminating": "Only after that diagnosis, predeclare a narrow additional stencil/high-precision experiment that can change a specified uncertainty. Adding h=.025 at representative signed points could provide a second R8 and more R4/R6 information, but is not automatically justified and is not a route to retroactively closing the old raw gate.",
    "raw_gate_feasibility": "Norm-only O(h^2) extrapolation suggests h around .002, but it ignores the persistent tiny-entry normalized maximum. Meeting the unchanged global component absolute branch by reducing the largest truncation error below1e-12 instead suggests h of a few1e-5 a0, where an O(epsilon/h) saved-sample sensitivity estimate can exceed1e-12. This is a feasibility warning, not a rigorous impossibility proof. No automatic .0015 or smaller-h run is justified.",
    "no_repeat": "Do not repeat the unchanged72-query ladder, lower the current acceptance criteria, zero/project D, or claim a physical identity proof.",
    "production": "New experimental acceptance and global derivative-error certification remain separate."
  },
